1
00:00:00,420 --> 00:00:02,442
Book Two, Proposition Eleven.

2
00:00:02,442 --> 00:00:12,201
To cut a given segment so that the rectangle contained by the whole and one of the segments is equal to the square on the remaining segment.

3
00:00:12,201 --> 00:00:19,730
Let A B be the given segment to be cut at a point H so that the rectangle A B, B H equals the square on A H.

4
00:00:20,150 --> 00:00:24,963
On A B, describe the square A B D C by One Forty-Six.

5
00:00:24,963 --> 00:00:28,686
Bisect A C at E by One Ten, and join B E.

6
00:00:28,686 --> 00:00:40,400
Produce C A to F, make E F equal to B E by One Three, describe the square F H on A F by One Forty-Six, and draw G H through to K.

7
00:00:40,820 --> 00:00:54,668
Since A C is bisected at E and F A is added to it, by Two Six the rectangle C F, F A together with the square on A E equals the square on E F, which equals the square on E B.

8
00:00:54,668 --> 00:01:07,879
By One Forty-Seven, the square on E B equals the squares on A B and A E; subtracting the square on A E from each leaves the rectangle F K equal to the square A B D C.

9
00:01:07,879 --> 00:01:16,634
Subtract the common rectangle A K from each; then the remaining square F H equals the remaining rectangle H D.

10
00:01:16,634 --> 00:01:24,592
Because A B equals B D, H D is the rectangle contained by A B and B H, and F H is the square on A H.

11
00:01:24,592 --> 00:01:32,949
Therefore, A B has been cut at H so that the rectangle contained by A B and B H equals the square on A H.

12
00:01:32,949 --> 00:01:34,620
Which was to be done.

13
00:01:35,040 --> 00:01:45,332
Proposition Two Eleven is Euclid's compass-and-straightedge construction of the Golden Section—later called extreme and mean ratio in Book Six, Proposition Thirty.

14
00:01:45,332 --> 00:02:00,487
Algebraically, it solves the quadratic equation a times the quantity a minus x equals x squared, or x squared plus a x equals a squared, without irrational numbers—unlocking the Pythagorean construction of the regular pentagon in Book Four.

15
00:02:00,487 --> 00:02:11,600
In the GEOMETOR G-Index, node G ninety-three synthesizes ten direct prerequisites across sixty-eight ancestor nodes, combining Proposition Two Six with the Pythagorean theorem.

16
